{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of stage S1, at exact head 0d1c7e11a3989bdfbaffeb60729be120821fdf86 on request/lane-worktree-identity (pushed).\n\nWhat governs it. Child request ac54fe71 (#22310), \"S1: deliver the governing design and read-only checkout association\", a stage child of #22168, itself governed by Hugh's effective adoption ba18cd1a of proposal 7a71886b (#22208) and the reconciled design. My promise on that child is a4c569b1. This head implements adopted layer 1 and the cleanup reporting that reads the same table, and nothing else. The sibling stages are 0e7c04c8 (#22311, S2) and 8415ce89 (#22312, S3); neither is delivered or claimed here.\n\nWhat to read first. The reporting artifact is the internal/app/association.go artifact 8d6e6b67775c435351ff13dd4666430654b71bb7, published last among the artifacts resting on the promise; it is the implementation report and states the conditions one by one, the proof-obligation to test table, the seventeen omission controls, the gates, and what S1 deliberately does not do. Sixteen companion artifacts stand at this same head, one per changed path: internal/gitstore/lineage.go, internal/gitstore/lineage_test.go, internal/gitstore/graph.go, internal/reviewguard/binding.go, internal/app/landing.go, internal/app/app.go, internal/app/worktree_landing.go, internal/app/worktree_index_test.go, internal/app/checkout_reporting_test.go, internal/app/association_test.go, internal/service/ui.go, internal/service/worktrees_remote_test.go, docs/reference/architecture.md, docs/reference/landing-observations.md and the two notes under notes/. Seventeen paths, seventeen artifacts, all at this one head.\n\nEvidence attached: gates-s1-v2.txt (the seven gate runs at this tree, each with its rc), gates-s1.txt (the runs at the predecessor head 593df535, which carry the race gate) and mutants-s1.txt (the seventeen omission controls, each with its guard, its mutation and the test that failed when the guard was gone).\n\nTwo evidence disclosures, made rather than left for you to find. First, the race gate is carried, not fresh: go test -race -count=1 ./internal/app/ ran rc=0 at 593df535, and the only Go change between that head and this one is a comment, so no instruction, no ordering and no shared state moved. Second, the seventeen omission controls were not re-run at this head for the same reason, so mutants-s1.txt describes 593df535, whose Go tree this head reproduces exactly apart from that comment. Say if you want either re-run at this head. Separately, an earlier run inside gates-s1.txt saw TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord in internal/docset fail twice with \"refuse verified frontier rollback\" because other sessions were appending to the live workroom while the gate read it; the docset gate is rc=0 in gates-s1-v2.txt.\n\nWhat this head changes over 593df535, in full: the WorktreeView comment in internal/app/app.go now points at association.go instead of the lane_identity.go the rename removed, and notes/2026-09-09-lane-identity-reconciled.md gains a \"Three wording questions, answered\" section before its proof obligations. Nothing else.\n\nWhat I am asking you to check.\n\n1. The grading. Five grades: claimed, corroborated, unresolved, foreign, unknown. Confirm corroboration requires a standing, effective, unretired artifact statement naming the exact commit plus reviewguard.OwnedEdge tying the signing actor to that same governing record (or AdoptedDecision for self-initiated work), that it attaches to a commit and never to a branch, and that a tip past the signed commit is demoted to claimed with corroborated_at naming the signed one.\n\n2. The forged-trailer control. A commit carrying a forged Rests-On trailer under a copied performer author name and email must stay claimed. Confirm no Git author ident is read, matched or mapped anywhere on this path, and that no second actor identity system appears.\n\n3. The read-only proof. Confirm no write exists in internal/app/association.go or internal/gitstore/lineage.go, including on every degraded input path (missing ref inventory, cancelled read, budget exhaustion, unknown objects), and that TestTheAssociationPassWritesNothing actually proves it -- it digests the whole repository either side of the pass -- rather than asserting it.\n\n4. Bounds and the cache key. 256 tips, 512 first-parent commits per tip (per tip, not global), the shared 65,536-step budget, the three-second deadline, the existing one-mebibyte commit-object cap, and the existing bounded ref inventory. The cache key pairs the durable frontier with a SHA-256 digest of the observed ref inventory, names and object ids both. Confirm exhaustion or cancellation discards the whole batch and marks every row unknown rather than returning a partial table, and that a hot cache cannot outlive a branch move, rename or deletion at an unchanged frontier.\n\n5. The pending-decision and cleanup controls. The structural rests_on edge is what is read; a retired artifact is still a proposal's subject; staleness is not settlement and an unknown lifecycle word protects; where two apply the earlier proposal identifier is named deterministically. Confirm the Case C counterexample is paired in the same fixture with a truly eligible candidate that must still be offered, so blanket refusal cannot pass. Confirm the four new reports protect and none deletes, and that no merge or retirement path is changed.\n\n6. Architecture paragraphs. docs/reference/architecture.md changes contract in the same head: layer 6 gains \"The pending-decision read adds no projected field\", layer 7 gains the reverse association and the four reports. Confirm the layer 6 claim is true -- no field, no statement kind, no admission rule, no fold profile advance, projection bytes unchanged -- and that the layer 7 text matches what the code actually does, including the derived checkout root and its over-protection-only argument.\n\n7. Landing-observations wire fields. Every field name the page states must match a Go json tag exactly, and a field the page does not name must be one it deliberately leaves out rather than one it misnames. The tags added at this head are: on the response, associations; on the table, rows, complete, reason; on a row, branch, checkouts, head, grade, governing, corroborated_at, claims, truncated; on a claim, commit, selector, governing, grade, artifact, actor, request, promise, decision, reason, candidates, candidates_omitted; on a checkout view, governing, grade, duplicate_head, refless_head, outside_checkout_root, symlinked_path, pending_decision. Note that ui/src/lib/api.ts is deliberately not extended and the wireparity gate does not cover WorktreeView, so no gate catches drift here; say whether you accept that as a stated S1 gap or want the mirror in this head.\n\n8. The three wording answers, which are the substantive addition at this head. The S1 request asks that three questions it carries forward from message 4128 be settled before this head lands. Message 4128 is ephemeral chat and not a durable record, so I could not read it; I took each question's substance from the durable stage requests ac54fe71 (#22310), 0e7c04c8 (#22311) and 8415ce89 (#22312), and wrote the answers against those and against the adopted proposal 7a71886b. Please check them there rather than against a message neither of us can open. Specifically: that the S2 answer keeps unknown distinct from a determinate negative and never lets an unreadable projection reach an authority check; that the short-selector refusal is correctly identified as extending the adopted refusal list rather than reading it, and so is named an S2 proposal item rather than settled here; that the S3 branch-spelling answer is true of validBranchRef and of schema-keyed admission, and rewrites no historical byte; that the S3 isolation answer does not overclaim -- it says the S3 landing buys attribution and a clean source revert, not operational rollback, because a reader that does not know a new schema rules such an event ineffective and reverting the source does not unwrite it. If any answer changes a semantic proposal 7a71886b fixed, that is a changes-requested finding.\n\n9. Stage boundaries. Confirm this head assigns no S2 or S3 work and claims none: no gs worktree command, no per-checkout record, no refs/gitseq/lanes ref, no branch field on any durable kind, no statement schema or fold profile change. Part C of the changes note asks the reviewer of each stage to confirm exactly this in the architecture conclusion, and that the parent #22168 stays open and is not closed or narrowed by this landing.\n\n10. One more point I want your judgement on rather than my own: internal/reviewguard/binding.go exports ownedEdge and adoptedDecision as OwnedEdge and AdoptedDecision so the association reader uses the same rules rather than a private copy. Confirm no review-guard decision moves.\n\nAlso: no CLI worktree surface exists in cmd/gs and S1 adds none. The only surface this head extends is the resident's GET /v0/worktrees and the WorktreeView rows it already carried.\n\nMerge base. This head is one commit over main 359b211c. Current main is 411681f1, twelve commits further on. git merge-tree --write-tree main 0d1c7e11 is clean, producing tree cdcad38099c617e8a477e9df759c3b7cd26ef4fb, so no rebase or recut is needed; the gap is ordinary staleness of the reasoning, not a changed head. Two paths gained live artifacts on other branches while this work was in flight -- docs/reference/architecture.md and internal/app/app.go -- and the artifact set rests on every live artifact at each path, so the merge plan can see them.\n\nFile the verdict with gs review, naming the internal/app/association.go artifact first, so the lane resolves through the reporting artifact. I will ratify the report.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8d6e6b67775c435351ff13dd4666430654b71bb7","conditions":"Independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of the exact head 0d1c7e11a3989bdfbaffeb60729be120821fdf86 on request/lane-worktree-identity, against child request ac54fe71 (#22310) under promise a4c569b1 and the adopted design ba18cd1a/7a71886b, reading the seventeen artifacts published at that head with the internal/app/association.go artifact as the reporting artifact. State the three conclusions explicitly. Architecture: name the affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md, say whether the head preserves or changes their contract, confirm the layer 6 and layer 7 paragraphs in this same head describe what the code does, and confirm the head neither delivers nor claims any outcome of stages 0e7c04c8 (#22311) or 8415ce89 (#22312) and does not close or narrow parent #22168. Security: examine the association's trust boundary -- an unsigned source Rests-On trailer as evidence and never authority, corroboration only from a standing effective artifact plus reviewguard's owned edge or adopted decision, no Git author ident read, the forged-trailer and copied-ident control, per-object hash re-verification before any trailer read, the exported OwnedEdge and AdoptedDecision changing no review-guard decision, the tip and per-tip commit bounds, the frontier-plus-ref-digest cache key, whole-batch discard on exhaustion or cancellation, the derived checkout root, the four protecting cleanup reports including the pending-decision Case C control paired with a truly eligible candidate, and that the whole path performs no write on any input. Simplification: identify any opportunity to simplify without weakening these conditions. Check the reconciled note's new \"Three wording questions, answered\" section against the adopted proposal 7a71886b and the durable stage requests ac54fe71, 0e7c04c8 and 8415ce89, since message 4128 is ephemeral chat that neither of us can read and the answers were written from those durable records instead: confirm no answer changes a semantic the proposal fixed, and that the short-selector refusal is correctly held out as an S2 proposal item rather than settled. File the verdict with gs review naming the internal/app/association.go artifact first. Attached: gates-s1-v2.txt, the seven gate runs at this tree with each rc; gates-s1.txt, which carries the race gate run at predecessor head 593df535; and mutants-s1.txt, the seventeen omission controls, which describe 593df535 because the only Go change since is a comment.","head":"0d1c7e11a3989bdfbaffeb60729be120821fdf86","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8d6e6b67775c435351ff13dd4666430654b71bb7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ac54fe712abc7ca0614ca1067bad1a7ff8c490b0 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:005460183c819676fead7d19fdd20633b48dc16c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d and 188 more","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}